/**
 * @defgroup Foundation_Workflows_ContinuousIntegration Continuous Integration
 * @ingroup Foundation_Workflows_Validation
 * @brief Native, documentation, and embedded GitHub Actions validation.
 *
 * `.github/workflows/native-ci.yml` executes Foundation's repository scripts on
 * native GitHub-hosted runners. The workflow YAML selects a runner and preset;
 * it does not reproduce the configure, build, test, export, or consumer logic
 * already owned by `scripts/`.
 *
 * @section FoundationContinuousIntegrationTriggers Triggers and Permissions
 *
 * Native CI runs for:
 *
 * - pull requests targeting `main`;
 * - pushes to `main`;
 * - explicit `workflow_dispatch` requests.
 *
 * It has read-only repository permission and uses no project secrets. A
 * concurrency group cancels an older run for the same branch or pull request
 * when a newer commit supersedes it. Matrix fail-fast is disabled so one
 * compiler failure does not hide the state of the other platforms.
 *
 * @section FoundationContinuousIntegrationMatrix Native Matrix
 *
 * | Runner | Preset | Compiler / target |
 * | ------ | ------ | ----------------- |
 * | `macos-15` | `macos_arm64` | Apple Clang, Arm64 |
 * | `ubuntu-24.04` | `linux_gcc_x64` | GNU GCC, x86-64 |
 * | `ubuntu-24.04` | `linux_clang_x64` | Clang, x86-64 |
 * | `ubuntu-24.04-arm` | `linux_gcc_arm64` | GNU GCC, Arm64 |
 * | `ubuntu-24.04-arm` | `linux_clang_arm64` | Clang, Arm64 |
 * | `windows-2025-vs2026` | `windows_msvc_x64` | MSVC, x86-64 |
 * | `windows-2025-vs2026` | `windows_clang_x64` | Clang, x86-64 |
 *
 * Runner versions are pinned deliberately instead of using moving `latest`
 * labels. Apple Clang is the native macOS toolchain. GNU GCC is validated on
 * Linux; `/usr/bin/gcc` on macOS is an Apple Clang driver, while Homebrew GCC
 * would be a separate compiler and ABI identity requiring its own preset and
 * support promise.
 *
 * @section FoundationContinuousIntegrationSequence Job Sequence
 *
 * Every matrix entry performs the same acceptance sequence:
 *
 * 1. report CMake, Ninja, compiler, OS, and architecture information;
 * 2. configure fresh, build, and run the registered suite in Debug;
 * 3. build and run the same suite in Release from the multi-config tree;
 * 4. perform a fresh Release export and verify its exact contents, metadata,
 *    compiler/ABI identity, and real archive architecture;
 * 5. recreate the standalone package consumer, find only that exported prefix,
 *    link `Foundation::Foundation`, and execute the consumer test;
 * 6. prove the identity gate rejects an added Debug archive, a missing public
 *    header, and modified build-identity metadata.
 *
 * Debug and Release do not name separate test suites. The same tests execute
 * against both library configurations. Debug remains a development artifact;
 * the exported and consumer-tested package contains Release only.
 *
 * Bash runners invoke:
 *
 * @code{bash}
 * ./scripts/test.sh <preset> --config Debug --fresh --parallel 4
 * ./scripts/test.sh <preset> --config Release --parallel 4
 * ./scripts/test-package.sh <preset> --fresh --parallel 4
 * @endcode
 *
 * Windows runners invoke the equivalent `.ps1` commands. Both test scripts
 * reject an empty CTest suite, and `test-package` forces a real archive link by
 * calling out-of-line Time symbols. See @ref Foundation_Workflows_Test and
 * @ref Foundation_Workflows_PackageConsumer.
 *
 * The native Arm64 runner is equivalent CPU/OS evidence for the Linux Arm64
 * package identity. It is useful preparation for a Raspberry Pi consumer, but
 * it does not prove Raspberry Pi board, kernel, peripheral, or deployment
 * behavior.
 *
 * @section FoundationContinuousIntegrationEmbedded Embedded Matrix
 *
 * `.github/workflows/embedded-ci.yml` cross-compiles every public AVR and Arm
 * preset on `ubuntu-24.04`. It installs the matching GNU toolchain, builds the
 * Foundation target in Debug and Release, performs a fresh Release export,
 * inspects every archive with target binutils, rejects Debug files in the
 * package, verifies the Release import mapping and build metadata, and uploads
 * the validated package for inspection.
 *
 * The matrix contains ATmega328P/AVR5; the four generic Cortex-M profiles;
 * PSoC 5LP; and the STM32F103, STM32F407, and STM32H743 identities. These jobs
 * are compile/package evidence only. They deliberately do not invoke CTest,
 * execute target code on the x86-64 runner, or claim firmware/hardware proof.
 *
 * @section FoundationContinuousIntegrationQuality Warnings and Sanitizers
 *
 * When `FOUNDATION_TESTING` is on, `foundation_enable_warnings` compiles the
 * Foundation library, every test executable, and the header self-containment
 * target with warnings as errors: `/W4 /permissive-` on MSVC and
 * `-Wall -Wextra -Wpedantic -Wshadow -Wconversion -Wsign-conversion
 * -Wold-style-cast -Wnon-virtual-dtor -Woverloaded-virtual` elsewhere. Every
 * native job therefore enforces the warning set. Consumer and embedded builds,
 * which do not enable testing, keep their own flags. Locally, pass
 * `--compile-no-warning-as-error` to CMake to downgrade the errors.
 *
 * The `sanitizers` job in `native-ci.yml` runs the Debug suite with
 * AddressSanitizer and UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer (`-fno-sanitize-recover=all`)
 * on Linux GCC and Clang x64 and on macOS Apple Clang Arm64. MSVC offers no
 * UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer and is not part of this job.
 *
 * @section FoundationContinuousIntegrationCoverage Coverage
 *
 * The `coverage` job in `native-ci.yml` builds the Debug suite with GCC on
 * Ubuntu x64 and `FOUNDATION_COVERAGE=ON`, then runs gcovr 8.6 over `src/` and
 * the staged include tree (header-only code is compiled from the staged copies).
 * Tests and GoogleTest are excluded. The job writes a line/function/branch
 * summary to the workflow page, uploads a browsable HTML report as the
 * `coverage-report` artifact, and fails when line coverage drops below 90%, the agreed 1.x threshold.
 *
 * To produce the same report locally with Apple Clang:
 *
 * @code{.sh}
 * cmake --preset macos_arm64 -DFOUNDATION_TESTING=ON -DFOUNDATION_COVERAGE=ON --fresh
 * cmake --build build/macos_arm64 --config Debug
 * ctest --test-dir build/macos_arm64 -C Debug
 * mkdir -p coverage
 * gcovr --gcov-executable "xcrun llvm-cov gcov" -r . build/macos_arm64 \
 *     --filter build/macos_arm64/include/ --filter src/ --html-details coverage/index.html
 * @endcode
 *
 * Baselines after `TEST-004`/`TEST-005`: GCC in CI measured 93.0% lines
 * (1347/1448), 98.9% functions, and 72.2% branches in
 * [GitHub Actions run 36227132453](https://github.com/ChecheRomo96/Foundation/actions/runs/36227132453);
 * Apple Clang locally measured 88.8% lines (1184/1334), 99.0% functions, and
 * 65.4% branches. The compilers count template and inline lines differently.
 *
 * @section FoundationContinuousIntegrationBoundaries Boundaries
 *
 * This native matrix does not claim the following evidence:
 *
 * - `macos_x64`, which requires an Intel runner or Rosetta execution and is
 *   validated locally through Rosetta 2 (VAL-002);
 * - Raspberry Pi-specific integration and hardware behavior;
 * - AVR, STM32, or PSoC firmware linkage and hardware behavior, and Arduino
 *   board execution (sketches are compiled, not run);
 * - branch-coverage gates; only line coverage is enforced.
 *
 * Those concerns retain separate backlog tasks. In particular, embedded jobs
 * must never report cross-compilation as target execution.
 *
 * @section FoundationContinuousIntegrationDocumentation Documentation Validation
 *
 * `.github/workflows/deploy-docs.yml` validates Doxygen 1.14.0 on pull requests
 * targeting `main`, pushes to `main` or `master`, and manual dispatches. The
 * validation job has read-only repository permission, performs a fresh build,
 * and uploads the generated HTML as a short-lived workflow artifact. A separate
 * deployment job runs only for push events, consumes that validated artifact,
 * and alone receives permission to update the `docs` branch. Doxygen uses
 * `WARN_AS_ERROR = FAIL_ON_WARNINGS`, so structural and reference warnings fail
 * validation before deployment.
 *
 * @section FoundationContinuousIntegrationFailureEvidence Failure Evidence
 *
 * Native Debug, Release, sanitizer, coverage, and package-consumer tests write
 * JUnit XML through the repository scripts. If a job fails, GitHub Actions
 * retains the available JUnit files, CTest `Testing/Temporary` logs, CMake
 * cache/configure logs, and any partially exported package for 14 days.
 * Feature-matrix failures retain the failing case's build tree. Embedded jobs
 * retain CMake diagnostics and any package prefixes produced before an identity
 * failure. Documentation failures retain the complete
 * captured build log and generated Doxyfile. Release packaging and complete-set
 * verification use the same failure-only retention policy.
 *
 * Failure artifacts are diagnostic evidence, not distributable packages. They
 * are uploaded only when the owning job fails and may be incomplete by design;
 * successful validated packages keep their existing shorter-lived artifact
 * names and policies.
 *
 * `.github/workflows/failure-evidence.yml` is a manual-only acceptance probe
 * for this policy. It is never triggered by a push or pull request. When
 * dispatched with `confirm_controlled_failure=true`, one Ubuntu/Bash job and
 * one Windows/PowerShell job create representative CMake, CTest, JUnit, and
 * partial-package diagnostics, then intentionally exit with code 23. Their
 * `if: failure()` steps upload separate 14-day artifacts. A final Ubuntu job
 * downloads both artifacts and verifies every expected file and marker. The
 * probe jobs use job-level `continue-on-error`, so an expected failure does
 * not make the acceptance workflow red; missing or malformed evidence still
 * fails the final verification job.
 *
 * Run the acceptance probe from **Actions → Verify CI Failure Evidence → Run
 * workflow**, enable the confirmation input, or dispatch it with:
 *
 * @code{bash}
 * gh workflow run failure-evidence.yml \
 *     --ref main \
 *     -f confirm_controlled_failure=true
 * @endcode
 *
 * Before each native matrix run, CI seeds representative stale build and
 * distribution files. It first verifies that normal clean removes only the
 * selected build tree, then that clean with the distribution flag removes both
 * repository-owned trees. It reseeds stale state so the Debug test's fresh
 * configure and the package consumer's fresh export must also remove it. The
 * final verification requires a newly configured cache, public umbrella
 * header, package config, and generated build metadata, so deletion alone
 * cannot produce a false pass.
 *
 * @section FoundationContinuousIntegrationAcceptance Acceptance and Evidence
 *
 * `CI-001` and `CI-002` were accepted for Foundation 1.2.0 by
 * [GitHub Actions run 36194411008](https://github.com/ChecheRomo96/Foundation/actions/runs/36194411008).
 * Its five published native identities completed the Debug and Release suites
 * plus fresh Release package-consumer validation. Main-branch run
 * [36227959990](https://github.com/ChecheRomo96/Foundation/actions/runs/36227959990)
 * subsequently passed all seven native identities, sanitizers, coverage, and
 * the feature matrix. Run
 * [36227959994](https://github.com/ChecheRomo96/Foundation/actions/runs/36227959994)
 * validated and deployed Doxygen, while run
 * [36227959989](https://github.com/ChecheRomo96/Foundation/actions/runs/36227959989)
 * built, exported, and inspected AVR and every declared Arm profile.
 *
 * `PKG-004` added exact package-content and binary-identity gates. All seven
 * native packages, their isolated consumers, and all three deliberate invalid
 * package mutations passed in
 * [run 36267812890](https://github.com/ChecheRomo96/Foundation/actions/runs/36267812890).
 * AVR and all eight declared Arm packages passed the same content/metadata gate
 * plus target-binutils architecture and float-ABI inspection in
 * [run 36267813048](https://github.com/ChecheRomo96/Foundation/actions/runs/36267813048).
 *
 * `PKG-005` added the `arduino` job. It compiles every example sketch for
 * `arduino:avr:uno` with the unmodified Arduino AVR core (C++11) and fails on
 * any Foundation warning. All 12 sketches passed in
 * [run 36270755264](https://github.com/ChecheRomo96/Foundation/actions/runs/36270755264).
 *
 * `CI-005` is implemented across every production workflow. Its manual
 * acceptance probe completed in
 * [run 36348973174](https://github.com/ChecheRomo96/Foundation/actions/runs/36348973174):
 * the expected Linux and Windows jobs failed, both `if: failure()` upload steps
 * succeeded, GitHub retained two distinct artifacts, and the final job
 * downloaded and verified both diagnostic sets. The overall acceptance run
 * succeeded because only the two explicit probe jobs tolerate failure.
 */
